WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.840
There are various estimates

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.420
of how big a drain on
the world's resources

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.520
the digital economy is.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.180
One data point is that 1% of
the world's carbon emissions

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.380
are created by people just
streaming video over their phones

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.850
or on their PCs.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.550
Smartphones, computers, data centers,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.490
and all the digital
activities we take for granted

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.750
are estimated to use around
7% of the world's electricity.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.520
And that's only going to get worse

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.550
as we rely more and more on our devices.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.230
Probably the best way to look at that

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.390
is what's going on in data centers.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.210
These are these giant
factories full of computers

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.860
that are processing

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.990
all of the information that you generate.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.240
These giant data centers

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.000
are on a course to be
using much more electricity

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.280
by perhaps the end of the decade.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.160
Clearly, that kind of a drain
is just not sustainable.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.240
We need new solutions.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.860
We need novel solutions.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.230
Scientists and startups around the world

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.840
are developing low-energy computer chips

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.090
which could be better for the environment

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:06.630
and upgrade the intelligence
of our digital devices.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.880
Everything's tied to
computers getting better.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.380
It could be finance,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.210
it could be chemical engineering,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.320
name your field.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.150
So my goal since founding the company

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.910
has been to develop a
new computing paradigm,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.590
a new technology platform,

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.190
that will allow computers to
continue to make progress.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.870
It's not only about design

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.990
but also sending them for fabrication,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.900
testing them and making system prototypes

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.880
for various application.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.890
The focus of the application

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.920
would be reducing the energy consumption

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.840
to the minimum level.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.530
It's a very high risk, high
reward type of problem.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.120
So if we're successful,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.270
then we'll transform this industry.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.513
That'd be this enormous
leap in this field.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.070
From space hardware to toasters,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.750
almost everything in modern life

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.113
depends on silicon-based
semiconductors called chips.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.430
They're the things that
make electronic items smart.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.200
At a very fundamental level,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.110
semiconductors are made up
of what's called transistors,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.400
and these are the absolutely
microscopic digital switches,

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.690
the on and offs.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.990
On or off means zero or
one in the digital realm.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.760
Say a modern graphics chip

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.570
will have tens of billions
of transistors on it

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.150
and this is something that's
the size of your thumbnail.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.760
So how do we get all
of these tiny switches

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.740
onto something relatively small?

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.820
Well, we do that by building
up layers of material

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.290
on a disk of silicon,

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.170
scraping off patterns
into those materials,

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.490
layering on other materials

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.040
until we have these tiny circuits

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.813
that give the chip its function.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.600
For half a century,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.850
the semiconductor industry has made chips

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.100
exponentially faster and better

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.560
by shrinking the size of transistors,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.580
turning room-sized computers
into pocket-sized smartphones,

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.130
propelling the digital revolution.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.530
Clearly, new technology is presenting

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.300
both management and staff

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.730
with an ever-growing range of choices

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.630
about how and where work
will be done in the future.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.100
But traditional computing

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.510
is reaching its limit.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.610
State-of-the-art mass produced chips

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.820
are made with what's called
5 nanometer technology,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.490
a dimension that smaller than a virus.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.530
And materials inside some devices

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.300
are already one atom thick,

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.940
meaning they can't be made any smaller.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.500
And we're reaching the limits of physics.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.320
We're reaching the
limits of energy density.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.080
The increasing consensus
in the chip industry

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.930
is that these advances
that we've got from silicon

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.880
are beginning to come to an end.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.190
So that if we really
want to take advantage

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.100
of the full potential of
artificial intelligence

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.650
and the absolute ocean of data

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.150
that we're all creating today,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.520
we're going to need new materials,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.500
we're going to need
new programming models,

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.173
we're going to need new
versions of computers.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.500
My personal first time about learning

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.050
and discovering what a carbon nanotube was

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:28.440
was when I was just about finishing up

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.000
college, undergraduate.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.370
And I saw a presentation

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:37.370
by the vice president of research of IBM

00:04:37.373 --> 00:04:39.963
and immediately I was,

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.140
thought it was very special.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.530
A carbon nanotube is a material

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:48.777
made entirely of carbon atoms.

00:04:48.777 --> 00:04:52.140
The carbon atoms are arranged in a tube.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.720
Imagine taking a sheet of carbon atoms

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.960
and rolling them into a tube

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.010
and then making that tube

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.860
smaller and smaller
and smaller and smaller

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.210
until the diameter of that
tube was just a few atoms.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.880
It's this extremely small,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.850
which is about 100,000 times

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.280
smaller than the diameter of an eyelash

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.150
and a hundred times smaller than a virus.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.560
The really cool thing about nanotubes

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.000
is that they conduct electricity

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.190
better than just about any other material

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:24.960
that's ever been discovered.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.130
Electrons will move along
the length of a nanotube

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.920
faster than they do in silicon.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.760
And that means you can get
ultimately faster switching

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.530
between on and off states.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:39.280
You can make faster computer chips.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.280
You could turn them on
and off with less voltage.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.410
And that means they use less electricity,

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.020
less power than silicon.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.040
In theory, nanotubes will be able to do

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.920
a thousand times better than silicon.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.200
Same computational capabilities,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.150
1,000 times less power.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.640
Another advantage is that carbon nanotubes

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.830
can be processed at a low temperature,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:08.670
so layers of nanotubes can be
built on top of one another.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.200
Silicon has to be processed

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.180
at an extremely high temperature,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.580
which makes 3D layers much harder.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.980
If you really think about a city,

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.050
what happens when you run
out of real estate in a city

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.190
is you build up,

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:21.120
you build skyscrapers,

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.980
you build into the third dimension.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.650
And so if you can't make
the transistors smaller,

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:27.570
then you could improve your computer chip

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:28.860
by making more transistors,

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.380
by making multiple layers of transistors

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.823
on top of other transistors.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.540
Since the 1990s

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.240
when nanotubes were
first invented in Japan,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.720
different methods have been
developed to mass produce them.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.930
But every process creates
two types of nanotubes,

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.990
metallic ones and semiconducting ones.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.850
A metallic nanotube like a copper wire,

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.800
it's stuck in the metallic state.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.170
You can't switch it,

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.700
and that kills a circuit.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.110
So we need only semiconducting nanotubes

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:02.283
to really make nanotube electronics work.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.060
So molecules and polymers

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.540
that can be mixed in with
the carbon nanotube powder,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.510
which is this tangled mess of nanotubes.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.270
And those molecules
and polymers will stick

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.310
to just the semiconducting ones

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:18.810
and not the metallic ones,

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.400
or they'll stick differently.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.460
And then you could sort the
nanotubes and separate them

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.950
based on these differences.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.080
Initially, in a powder,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.070
there's about 67% of the
nanotubes are semiconducting.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.170
But using these chemical approaches,

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.430
we could extract over 99.99%

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.130
of the semiconducting nanotubes.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.150
After the semiconducting nanotubes

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.170
have been extracted,

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.390
they float around in a solution,

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.880
and so the next challenge
is to line them up neatly

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.260
on a silicon wafer,

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.693
which can then be turned
into a computer chip.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.830
Carbon nanotubes really
got me hooked and excited

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.060
right from the beginning.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:58.500
I mean they really have the opportunity

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.400
to potentially revolutionize electronics.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.500
This challenge of aligning them

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:08.130
in these highly packed,
highly aligned arrays

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.500
has really been frustrating

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.350
the field of carbon nanotube electronics

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.613
since their discovery.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.100
During my PhD,

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:20.460
I was kind of tasked with
actually determining,

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.540
you know, how we can better
align carbon nanotubes.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.360
We just found, you know,
kind of by accident

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.170
that when we layer carbon
nanotube ink or inks

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:34.560
where carbon nanotubes
are dissolved in solutions

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:35.940
like organic solvents,

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.150
if we layer that carbon
nanotube ink on water,

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:42.150
then instantaneously
those carbon nanotubes

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:46.260
will collect and confine at
that ink water interface.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:51.240
And that high ordering and
induced ordering by themselves

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.243
really helps to align
the carbon nanotubes.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.120
What you're seeing here is a aligned array

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.380
of hundreds of thousands
of carbon nanotubes.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.670
As you can see, individual nanotubes,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.890
these light-colored regions,

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:14.550
and then the dark-colored
region is the substrate.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:15.870
They're all really well-aligned

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:17.130
with respect to each other.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.730
Occasionally, there's a
misaligned nanotube in this,

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:24.000
then we need to improve
our manufacturing process

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.073
to eliminate those instances.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.160
The biggest current challenge

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.370
is being able to achieve
that high alignment

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.430
in extremely uniform arrays

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:39.150
across, you know, 300-millimeter wafers,

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.330
which is an industry
standard of silicon wafers,

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:45.330
before they start changing silicon

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:47.673
to integrate carbon nanotubes instead.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.430
If we can overcome these challenges,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.980
if we can make these
aligned nanotube wafers,

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:54.360
the major players in industry

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.500
will really jump into this field

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:56.333
and it could just

00:09:56.333 --> 00:09:58.280
progress at that point
could be very rapid.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.140
The technique isn't perfect yet,

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:05.370
but it's already an advance
for carbon nanotube research.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.470
Michael and Katherine
have founded a company

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.470
with an aim to solve remaining challenges.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.330
But many more breakthroughs are needed

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:13.980
before nanotube transistors

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.830
have even a chance of replacing silicon.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:18.390
It absolutely shows promise,

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:20.880
but it's been showing
promise for 20 years.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.200
There are many issues,

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.690
you know, how robust are these devices,

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.380
but more importantly can
you manufacture them?

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:29.880
The semiconductor industry

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.460
is based on silicon transistors,

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:34.800
and enormous sums have
already been invested

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:35.670
in infrastructure

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.340
to manufacture that technology.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.980
Plants have a price tag of $20 billion,

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:44.340
take years to build and need
to be run for 24 hours a day

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:45.960
to turn a profit.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.310
Change will be difficult

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.820
without a guarantee that carbon
nanotubes will be cheaper.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.100
Silicon's been around a long time.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:54.270
People know how to use it,

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:55.470
they know how to program it,

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.510
they know how to mass manufacture it.

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.930
It's still, in terms of
the economics, the winner.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.660
And until those economics change,

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.210
nothing is going to replace it.

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.060
I've always been obsessed with computers.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.370
You know, I had an opportunity
to work in industry

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.170
at a large semiconductor company

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.030
for a number of years.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:23.550
And I got to see there some
of the fundamental challenges

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.550
associated with continuing
to shrink transistors

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:30.810
and I found that to be not
a very exciting future.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:33.300
So my goal with Lightmatter
since founding the company

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:36.090
has been to develop a
new computing paradigm,

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.410
a new technology platform

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:41.640
that allows you to do calculations
using photonic devices.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.710
When electronics uses electrons,

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.290
that's the medium of transfer,

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.510
that's what represents the data.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:50.760
Photonics is using photons,

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.250
which is the basic constituency of light.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.430
So for example, a fiber optic cable

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:58.290
that spans the Pacific Ocean

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.200
is using light to transmit information.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.160
Why is it using light?

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.500
Well, it's because the energy required

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:07.320
to shove electrons along a copper cable

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:08.880
would be absolutely enormous,

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.590
and you would just have
too much signal decay.

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:13.590
So if you can convert that
information into photons,

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:17.220
you can send it faster and
with less energy overhead.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.383
So that's obviously a desirable thing.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.760
There's nothing faster
than the speed of light.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.200
So when you think about the latency

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:26.640
between when you make a query

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:29.010
and when a photonic computer
gives you an answer,

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:30.450
it's really the fundamental limit

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.730
for how fast you could do these things.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:33.780
Electrical wires,

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:34.830
there's a certain amount of time

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.630
that it takes for them to turn on and off.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:38.070
And every time you do,

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:39.840
it takes a lot of energy to do this,

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.850
to turn this wire off and on.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:45.090
Now with a photonic computer
and photonics in general,

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:49.350
you have a type of wire that
doesn't take very long at all,

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.410
femtoseconds maybe, even
attoseconds to turn on.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:56.280
It takes almost no energy other
than any light that's lost

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:59.763
while the optical signal is
propagating through that wire.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:02.760
You have another great
property with photonics

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.520
which is that you can
send multiple data streams

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.140
through this photonic wire,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.660
encoded in different
colors or wavelengths,

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:10.950
all at the same time.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:14.940
So you have massively parallel
computation and communication

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.013
through these nearly perfect wires.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:21.030
The idea of silicon photonics

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.690
has been around again for a long time.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:27.720
The problem comes with changing

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:31.440
those electrons-based
signals into photons.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.810
So the traditional way of doing that

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:34.980
is to have all of these
other pieces of equipment

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:38.040
that are expensive and use
lots of energy to do that.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.510
Silicon photonics is,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:41.730
well, maybe we can just design a chip

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:45.750
that directly deals in
both electrons and photons

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:49.440
that we can have, you know,
the benefits of both worlds.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.780
And that's what was supposedly
on the cusp of doing.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:54.210
Again, it's been promised for a long time,

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.380
it's gonna change the world,

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:58.200
or has been about to change
the world for a long time,

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.050
and it hasn't yet.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:01.860
But Lightmatter still believes

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.230
silicon photonics is going to happen,

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.750
and it's harnessing
existing laser technologies

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:08.040
used in the telecoms

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.710
and semiconductor processing industries.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.950
Their chips are specifically
being made for AI applications

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.620
like chat bots and self-driving cars.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.560
The startup plans to launch
their first photonic computer

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.150
later this year.

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:23.730
One of their current
products is an interconnect,

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:26.700
which enables chips to talk to each other.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.160
It could help solve
energy consumption issues

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:30.090
in data centers

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.790
and potentially bring down costs.

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:34.230
In these huge factories,

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:36.270
processors are closely arranged together

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.970
so they can communicate at high speeds.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.823
But this also generates
a massive amount of heat.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.270
So not only do you have
to put the power in

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:46.620
to power all of these components,

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.630
but then you have to use
an enormous amount of power

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:49.740
to actually cool them down

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:52.290
to stop them literally melting.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:54.540
What this company is trying to do

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.280
is to come up with interconnects

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.260
and ways of connecting components

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:03.259
that use light rather
than electrons over wires.

00:15:03.259 --> 00:15:04.620
So that means, in theory,

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:07.050
that you could have a
processor in one room,

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.960
memory in another, storage in another,

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.420
and that would help with the density

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:14.700
and the power and the heat.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.320
And that's definitely a solution

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.630
that's being tried by numerous companies,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.090
but we're still waiting for that to show,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:22.920
you know, practical everyday results

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.093
and be widely adopted.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:34.590
Human brain is very efficient.

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:37.170
Our brain operates all the time.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:41.700
It doesn't dissipates
more than 10 to 20 watts,

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.250
which is very small value.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.460
To solve the problem of Rubik's Cube,

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.170
so the amount of energy

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:54.170
which is required to learn
and then operate is very low

00:15:54.175 --> 00:15:58.350
as compared to the actual systems.

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:00.060
Just solving the problem,

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.620
put learning aside,

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:06.570
that itself will need
thousands of processors

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.130
to become parallel

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:11.010
and then dissipate a lot of power,

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.520
which can go beyond megawatt.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:15.900
These scientists in India

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.300
have been trying to build low-energy chips

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:21.780
mimicking the way the human
brain works for years.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.240
The human brain is
packed with neuron cells

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:27.060
that communicate with each
other through electrical pulses

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:28.440
known as spikes.

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:31.590
Each neuron releases molecules
that act as messengers

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.450
and control if the electrical pulse

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:35.460
is passed along the chain.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:38.250
This transmission happens
in the gaps between neurons,

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:39.753
which are called synapses.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.330
Neurons and synapses,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.430
they are fundamentally different

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:47.190
from the way computers compute.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.130
Computers use transistors,
which are binary.

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:52.380
They do zero, one, on, off.

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:55.950
The brain sort of does a similar thing

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:59.190
except that it works in analog.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.470
It's also partly stochastic.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.170
A neuron may spike or may not spike

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:08.073
depending upon inherent
internal randomness.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.240
The brain itself doesn't
like to do repetitive things.

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:15.690
It likes to do everything
with slight variance.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:18.120
This sort of enables creativity.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:20.503
It sort of enables problem solving.

00:17:20.503 --> 00:17:22.320
And the youngsters' creative imagination

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:23.253
is not neglected.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:27.341
Once there was a bear that was
twice the size of the earth

00:17:27.341 --> 00:17:28.470
and lived on the sun.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.120
The connections are different.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.370
So if you look at a computer chip,

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:36.900
every transistor roughly
talks to about 10 transistors.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:38.760
Every neuron, on the other hand,

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.400
talks to about 10,000 other neurons,

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.703
and that's literally what
makes it super parallel.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.570
Neurons use time as a
token of information.

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:50.820
Computers don't understand time per se,

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.310
they just sort of, you know,
work with digital numbers

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:54.993
and do mathematical operations.

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.900
To mimic the architecture of our brain

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:01.920
more closely,

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:04.500
the team designed an artificial neural net

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:08.130
based on the biological
neural networks in our brain.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:09.390
Each artificial neuron

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:11.490
is built with a few silicon transistors

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.530
and connected to other neurons.

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:16.320
We try to mimic the auditory cortex.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:19.770
So the auditory cortex has this neurons

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.440
which are connected in random.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:23.370
And that sounds weird,

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:26.070
but it turns out it serves
a very important function,

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.813
which is in speech processing.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:32.610
What we are doing is that there
are recurrent connections,

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:36.120
which means that the neuron
could go to any neuron

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.980
including itself,

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:41.100
creating some sort of loopy pathways,

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.900
which are fairly random.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:45.750
And these loops are part
of the architecture,

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.370
they are called liquid state machine.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:50.490
They naturally are able
to process time series,

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.440
like when you hear speech,

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:54.900
which is sort of occurs in time.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.790
We are doing some analog compute.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.130
There is some noise also in the neurons,

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:02.280
so there could be some stochasticity.

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:05.940
In the traditional or
conventional computation platform,

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:07.620
everything is digital.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.860
Everything is defined based
on logic one and zero.

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:12.390
Analog computers,

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:16.380
which are eventually signals
which can have any value,

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:19.920
would be able to get more complexity

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.150
at the same time more
functionality in the operation.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:25.890
Here we have our neural network chip

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:27.150
which is on the test board.

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:29.010
So what we'll do is we'll
speak to the computer,

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:32.790
and that will sort of convert
the speech into spikes.

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:34.950
It's gonna get processed in the chip.

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:36.480
We run the code.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:37.313
One.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:43.413
So it detects a one.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:45.720
This neuromorphic chip

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.490
has been able to recognize speech

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.643
using a fraction of the energy
used by a traditional chip.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.550
Just like biological neurons,

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:55.710
the artificial neurons wait for inputs

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.020
and only spike when data comes in,

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.243
and that means lower energy consumption.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:03.540
Each neuron is also highly efficient

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:05.640
by using a quantum mechanics phenomenon

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:07.950
called band-to-band tunneling.

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:10.980
So electrons are still able
to pass through transistors

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:12.670
with very low electric current.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.410
Quantum tunneling is nothing

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:20.010
that humans essentially
have experience for.

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:24.930
If you have a human being
that has to cross over a hill,

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:26.580
the human being has to walk up the hill,

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.020
burn energy,

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:31.230
and then walk down,
which again burns energy.

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:34.290
And so there is no way for
you to cross this barrier

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:36.360
without using up energy.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:38.490
If you make this hill thinner and thinner,

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:39.660
narrower and narrower

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.460
until it goes to the size scale

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:43.920
on the order of, say, a
few electron wavelengths,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.300
at which point,

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:47.370
an electron doesn't need
to go above the barrier

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:48.660
to surmount it,

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:51.210
it can, in principle, tunnel through it,

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:54.330
which means it will sort
of disappear on one side

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:56.280
and appear on the other side magically.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.220
If you take a transistor,

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:02.100
there's this same idea
that when it turns on,

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.980
we have a barrier that we sort of reduce,

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.720
and when we want to turn it off,

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:07.890
we increase the barrier.

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:10.740
That's when you expect
no current to go through,

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:15.740
but that is not true by quantum mechanics.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.520
So even through a barrier,

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:19.500
when it's narrow enough,

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:22.450
electrons can sort of, quote
and quote, "tunnel" through it.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:26.700
So far, there are 36 artificial neurons

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:28.050
on the chip,

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:30.690
and their energy consumption
is remarkably close

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:32.550
to biological neurons.

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:36.600
If we want to approach
the number of neurons

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:38.910
which are already in human brain,

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.250
so we need billions of them,

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:45.360
and therefore we need to
reduce the energy per spike

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:48.933
generated by a single
neuron as much as possible.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.440
Intel, one of the world's
biggest chipmakers,

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:01.980
has been working on brain-inspired
chips for seven years.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.360
They've released two versions

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:06.330
of their neuromorphic research chip Loihi.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:09.960
The latest one is only 32
square millimeters in size

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:12.153
but contains up to a million neurons.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:15.480
The company said Loihi has demonstrated

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:17.160
multiple applications,

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:19.500
including recognizing hazardous odors

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:22.140
and enabling robotic arms to feel.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:23.130
In the near term,

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:26.280
based on the most recent
results we've had with Loihi,

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:29.220
I would say that solving
optimization problems

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.980
are the most exciting application.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.410
Things like planning, navigating,

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:37.350
you can think of it of solving
the shortest path in a map,

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:40.260
for example, if you want to
get from point A to point B.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:41.940
These kind of optimization problems

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.940
are conventionally really compute heavy,

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:47.160
but we found that on a
neuromorphic architecture,

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:50.040
we can reduce the computational demands

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.540
by orders of magnitude.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.610
So over a 10 times speed up

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:56.433
as well as over a thousand
times energy reduction.

00:22:58.260 --> 00:22:59.910
Probably the majority of the groups

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:01.410
that are working with Loihi

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:04.290
sees robotics as kind of the long-term,

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.930
best application domain
for neuromorphic chips.

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:10.620
After all, brains evolved
in nature to control bodies

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:13.260
and to deal with real-world stimulus,

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:14.820
respond to it in real time,

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:17.220
and adapt to the, you
know, unpredictable aspect

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:18.480
of the real world.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:20.520
And if you think about robotics today,

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:24.240
they perform very
rigidly prescribed tasks.

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:27.420
But if you want to deploy a
robot into the real world,

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:29.520
we really don't have the technology yet

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:31.410
to make them as agile,

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.570
as adaptive as we really would like.

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:36.210
And so that's where we
see neuromorphic chips

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:37.323
really thriving.

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:39.870
Intel and its collaborators

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:42.630
have used the Loihi chip to power iCub,

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:44.580
a humanoid robot designed to learn

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.590
in a way that children might.

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:47.430
This iCub robot

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:49.860
is intended to interact
with the environment

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:51.900
as opposed to being pre-trained

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:55.080
with static data sets beforehand.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.270
So using kind of saccadic motions,

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:58.650
moving the eyeballs,

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:00.930
it can trace through and detect change

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:04.110
or edges in the objects
in front of this robot.

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:06.630
And using a fully neuromorphic paradigm,

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.610
we're able to design a network

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:12.240
that understands and
learns new object classes,

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:14.910
even at different angles
and different poses.

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:16.530
It adds to its understanding,

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:20.340
it's kind of dictionary of
understand objects over time.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:21.790
What is this called?

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:24.543
That's a mouse.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:27.270
Oh, I see.

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:28.380
This is a mouse.

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:29.670
Let me have a look at that.

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:30.503
Sure.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:34.383
Can you show me the mouse?

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:38.640
Here is the mouse.

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:48.443
So far

00:24:48.443 --> 00:24:51.390
all of this technology
is at an early stage.

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:53.040
But to fight climate change

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:55.500
and power the progress of civilization,

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:58.000
we need at least one of
them to be a breakthrough.

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:01.590
Greener chips absolutely have to happen.

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:04.230
People are concerned about
the environmental impact,

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.330
people are concerned about the efficiency,

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:08.100
people are concerned about the results

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:11.670
and the way that chips
can impact modern life.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:12.750
So there's been,

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:15.720
you know, an absolute
blossoming of investment

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:18.390
in chip startups and trying these things.

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:21.120
This is definitely the
time of great ideas.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:22.680
Will they all be successful?

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:23.670
Absolutely not.

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:25.290
But now, I would say more than ever,

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.120
there is a chance that out there

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:28.530
in some corner of the world,

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:32.250
there is a chip startup
that will be everywhere

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:34.113
within maybe the next 10 years.

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:37.950
You can't get your enjoyments

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:40.800
from only the successes in science.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.240
You need to get your enjoyment

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:45.660
from just enjoying solving hard problems.

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:47.430
And so if you fail,

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:49.530
that is just part of the process

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.543
of eventually finding a solution.

